The `render()` method was allocating a new intermediate framebuffer for each non-empty line on every invocation, causing repeated heap allocations in a frequently-called code path.

## Changes

- Added `line_buffer` member to `Impl` struct for buffer reuse across render calls
- Modified buffer management to resize only when dimensions change, otherwise clear in-place
- Replaced local `std::vector<unsigned char> line_fb` allocations with persistent buffer

## Implementation

```cpp
struct FourLineDisplayImpl::Impl {
    std::unique_ptr<FtText> small_ft;
    std::unique_ptr<FtText> large_ft;
    std::unique_ptr<MonoGfx> gfx;
    std::vector<unsigned char> line_buffer;  // Reusable buffer for line rendering
};

// In render():
const size_t buffer_size = static_cast<size_t>(available_width * (height_ / 8));
if (impl_->line_buffer.size() != buffer_size) {
    impl_->line_buffer.resize(buffer_size, 0);
} else {
    std::fill(impl_->line_buffer.begin(), impl_->line_buffer.end(), 0);
}
```

The optimization maintains correctness while eliminating allocation overhead in the common case where display dimensions remain constant between calls.

